    "stdout": "{\"name\":\"grit\",\"version\":\"[VERSION]\",\"path\":\".agents/skills/grit/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"---\\nname: grit\\ndescription: Use the grit CLI for version control in Git repositories — status, diffs, commits, branches, merges, cherry-picks, reverts, fetch/pull/push and tags — with JSON output for scripts and agents. Use when a task involves committing, branching, syncing with a remote or reading history and `grit` is installed.\\n---\\n\\n# grit\\n\\n`grit` is a small, opinionated Git client. It reads and writes ordinary Git repositories, so `git` and `grit` can be used on the same repository. It does not mirror Git's commands or flags: there is usually one obvious way to do each thing, and every command can answer in JSON.\\n\\nThis skill was generated by `grit [VERSION]`. Run `grit <command> --help` for the exact options of the installed version.\\n\\n## Rules\\n\\n- Pass `--json` whenever you will read the result. stdout then holds exactly one JSON object; progress and prompts go to stderr.\\n- Use `--filter '<jq-like expr>'` (with `--json`) to keep only what you need: `grit status --json --filter '{branch, clean}'`.\\n- On failure the exit code is 1 and stdout is `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"…\\\"}`. Exit code 2 is a usage error (unknown flag or command) and has no JSON.\\n- Always give `grit commit` a message (`-m`, a positional argument, or `-F` / `--message-file`, with `-` for stdin). It never opens an editor. Use `--author \\\"Name <email>\\\"` to override the author once; `--no-verify` skips `pre-commit` and `commit-msg`. Use `--dry-run` to preview paths, parents, and author without writing objects or updating refs (`--json` omits `oid` and sets `dry_run: true`).\\n- Don't translate Git habits flag-for-flag. `grit commit -a`, `git add -p`, and `reset` don't exist here; use `grit commit --amend` to replace the tip commit and `grit rebase` for replay onto another base (see \\\"What grit doesn't do\\\" for the rest).\\n\\n## Where am I?\\n\\n```console\\n$ grit status --json\\n```\\n\\nPlain `grit` is the same as `grit status`. Keys: `branch`, `detached`, `head`, `target` (the branch your work is headed for), `ahead`, `commits` (up to ten not on the target; each commit row includes `change_id` and `change_id_stored`), `staged`, `unstaged`, `untracked`, `clean`.\\n\\nThe target branch is the first that exists of: the `target.branch` config value, `origin/master`, `origin/main`, `master`, `main`. Change it with `grit config target.branch origin/develop`.\\n\\n## Reading history and changes\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Uncommitted changes as a diff | `grit diff` |\\n| Changed paths only | `grit diff --name-only` |\\n| Per-file line counts | `grit diff --stat` or `grit diff --numstat` |\\n| The change one commit made | `grit show <commit>` (patch by default; merge commits use combined diffs unless `--diff-merges=first-parent`) |\\n| Several commits at once | `grit show <A> <B>` (`--json` → `{ \\\"objects\\\": [ … ] }`) |\\n| Blob or tree at a revision | `grit show <rev>:<path>` (same resolution as `grit cat`) |\\n| A commit's message and diffstat only | `grit show [<commit-or-branch-or-tag>] --stat` |\\n| Recent history, ten at a time | `grit log`, then `grit log --before <next>` using the `next` value |\\n| History with patches or file lists | `grit log -p`, `grit log --stat`, `grit log --name-status`, … (same format flags as `grit diff`) |\\n| Commits on this branch not on the target | `grit shortlog` |\\n\\n`grit diff --json` on **`--staged`** or **`--unstaged`** patch output includes stable **`hunks[].id`** (12 lowercase hex chars) and **`hunks[].index`** (0-based within the file). Ids hash side, path, and line bodies (not line numbers), so they stay valid when earlier hunks are staged. Use **`grit add --hunks <id,...>`** or **`grit add --lines path:start-end`** (new-side **`lines[].new`**, 1-based inclusive); commit-range diffs omit `id` for now.\\n\\n**Partial staging:** `grit diff --unstaged --json -- path | jq '.files[].hunks[] | .id'` → `grit add --hunks id1,id2 -- path`, or pick **`lines[].new`** → `grit add --lines path:10-15 -- path`. Mix `--hunks` and `--lines`. One-shot commit: **`grit commit --hunks id1,id2 -m msg -- path`** or **`grit commit --lines path:10-15 -m msg -- path`**. Shelve selected unstaged hunks without touching the index: **`grit stash push --hunks id1,id2`** (optional **`--dry-run`**). Use `--dry-run` to preview. Exit code **2** for unknown/stale ids or invalid line ranges.\\n\\n**Partial discard / unstage:** same ids or line ranges on **`grit restore --hunks`** / **`grit restore --lines`** (default: worktree; add **`--staged`** to unstage selected hunks). **`--dry-run`** previews without writing.\\n\\nRevisions can be full or short ids, branch or tag names, or expressions like `HEAD~2`.\\n\\nCommit rows in `--json` from `log`, `show`, `revs`, and `status` share `change_id` (reverse-hex) and `change_id_stored`. Human `log` and `show` prefix each commit with `change <8 chars>`; `--oneline` does not.\\n\\n## Committing\\n\\n```console\\n$ grit commit -m \\\"Explain what changed and why\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`grit commit` **auto-stages** the whole worktree by default (`commit.autoStage=true`). Use **`grit commit --staged`** to commit only the index (set `commit.autoStage=false` to make that the default). Use **`grit commit -m msg -- <paths>`** (or `--pathspec-from-file`) for a partial commit that leaves other staged changes alone — like `git commit --only`. `-a` / `--all` forces auto-stage; `--staged` and paths cannot be combined with each other or with `-a`.\\n\\n**Partial hunks:** `grit diff --unstaged --json` → pick `hunks[].id` → **`grit commit --hunks id1,id2 -m msg`** (optional `-- paths`). Same exit code **2** as `grit add --hunks` for unknown or stale ids. **`grit commit --patch-file file`** applies a cached patch then commits. Neither mode auto-stages the rest of the tree.\\n\\n### Staging (`grit add`)\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Stage everything (modified, deleted, untracked) | `grit add` |\\n| Stage only tracked edits and deletions | `grit add -u` (or `grit add --update`) |\\n| Preview without writing the index | `grit add --dry-run` (combine with `-u` or paths) |\\n| Record intent to add (empty index slot, no blob yet) | `grit add -N -- path` (requires paths; does not alter tracked entries) |\\n| Stage an ignored path | `grit add -f -- ignored.log` |\\n| Stage specific paths | `grit add -- path/to/file` |\\n| Apply a unified diff to the index | `grit add --patch-file file.diff` (or `-` for stdin; like `git apply --cached`) |\\n| Stage selected diff hunks by id | `grit add --hunks <id,...>` (from `grit diff --json`; optional `-- paths`) |\\n| Stage part of a hunk by line range | `grit add --lines path:start-end` (new-side lines from `grit diff --json`; mix with `--hunks`) |\\n\\n`grit add --json` returns `{added, modified, removed, intent_to_add, dry_run, count, staged}` with repository-relative paths. Use `--dry-run` before bulk staging to bound agent work; path lists are sorted and stable. With `--patch-file`, paths come from the patch (same JSON fields as path-based staging).\\n\\n`grit commit` fails with \\\"nothing to commit\\\" on a clean tree unless you pass `--allow-empty`. On detached HEAD it advances `HEAD` without moving a branch; use `grit switch -c <name>` to keep the commits on a new branch.\\n\\nEach commit gets a stable **`change-id`** header (on by default) that survives `--amend`. Disable with `grit config grit.changeIds false` when you need Git-identical commit objects. See the docs site section on change ids for derivation and testing config.\\n\\n## Branches\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| List branches | `grit branch` |\\n| List with filters / JSON detail | `grit branch --list 'feat*' -a --merged main --sort date -n 20 --json` |\\n| Current branch name only | `grit branch --show-current` or `grit resolve --abbrev-ref HEAD` |\\n| Upstream tracking branch name | `grit resolve --abbrev-ref '@{u}'` (full: `grit resolve --symbolic-full-name '@{u}'`) |\\n| Create a branch at the current commit (no switch) | `grit branch <name>` |\\n| Create at a specific commit | `grit branch <name> <start>` |\\n| Create with upstream tracking | `grit branch -t <name> <remote>/<branch>` or rely on `branch.autoSetupMerge` |\\n| Rename a branch | `grit branch -m [<old>] <new>` or `grit branch -M <new>` (force) |\\n| Set / clear upstream | `grit branch -u <upstream> [<name>]`, `grit branch --unset-upstream [<name>]` |\\n| Preview any branch mutation | add `--dry-run --json` (no ref, config, or reflog changes) |\\n| Create and switch | `grit switch -c <name>` |\\n| Switch | `grit switch <name>` (also `grit checkout`, `grit co`) |\\n| Delete a merged branch | `grit branch -d <name>` |\\n| Delete regardless of merge | `grit branch --force-delete <name>` (short `-D`) |\\n| Predict merge conflicts (no repo changes) | `grit merge --check <branch>` (exit 0 = clean/ff/up to date, 1 = would conflict; `--json` → `result: clean\\\\|conflicts\\\\|…`) |\\n\\n`grit branch --json` and `--markdown` list with `full_ref`, `oid`, `subject`, `date`, optional `upstream` (`ref`, `remote`, `gone`, `ahead`, `behind`), and `worktree` when checked out elsewhere. Human list output adds an upstream marker when configured.\\n\\n`grit switch`, `grit merge`, `grit pick`, `grit revert` and `grit pull` refuse to run when checkout would overwrite local edits or untracked files. Preflight with `grit switch <branch> --dry-run --json` (checks only; reports `changed_paths`) before switching. On refusal, `--json` emits `kind: would_overwrite` with `reason` (`local_changes` or `untracked`) and sorted `paths`.\\n\\n## Combining work\\n\\n- `grit merge <branch>` fast-forwards when it can, otherwise records a merge commit. `<branch>` can be local or remote-tracking (`origin/main`). Use `grit merge --check <branch>` to predict conflicts in memory without touching refs, the index, or the worktree. Common flags: `--ff-only` (exit 128 when diverged), `--no-ff`, `--squash` (writes `SQUASH_MSG`; finish with `grit commit`), `--no-commit` (Git merge state; finish with `grit commit`), `-X ours|theirs`, `--conflict diff3|zdiff3|merge`, `--allow-unrelated-histories`, `-m` / `--message-file`.\\n- `grit pick [<commit>…]` cherry-picks commits onto the current branch (ranges OK). Use `grit pick --check` to predict conflicts without changing refs, the index, or the worktree (exit 0 = clean, 1 = would conflict or empty stop). `-X ours|theirs` auto-resolves replay conflicts; `--conflict diff3|zdiff3|merge` sets marker style when a step stops.\\n- `grit revert [<commit>…]` undoes one or more commits (ranges OK; newest first). Merge commits need `--mainline <n>`. `-m` / `--message-file` set the message for a **single** commit only. `grit revert --check` matches pick’s `--check` contract; same `-X` and `--conflict` flags apply.\\n- `grit revert --continue`, `--abort`, and `--skip` resume or cancel a stopped revert sequence (same conflict model as pick).\\n- On a conflict these commands update the index and working tree with conflict markers, exit 1, and leave merge/pick/revert/rebase state on disk. To inspect a side or recreate markers without resolving, use `grit restore --ours`, `--theirs`, or `--merge` on the conflicted paths (index stays unmerged). Fix conflicts (`grit diff`), stage (`grit add`), then finish with `grit commit` for merges or `grit pick --continue` / `grit revert --continue` / `grit rebase --continue`. Use `--abort` on the same command to cancel; `grit rebase --skip` skips the current commit.\\n\\n## History rewrites\\n\\n- `grit history drop`, `move`, `reword`, `fixup`, `squash`, and `absorb` share `--dry-run`, `--update-refs`, `--empty=(drop|keep|abort)` (empty replayed commits), and `--keep-committer` (preserve each replayed commit’s committer line).\\n- `grit history fixup` accepts **`--hunks`** and **`--lines`** like `grit add` to fold selected unstaged edits into a historical commit without staging the whole file; optional `-- paths` narrows the diff. Without those flags, fixup uses the staged index (same as before).\\n\\n## Remotes\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Copy a repository | `grit clone <url> [<dir>]` |\\n| List or add remotes | `grit remote`, `grit remote add <name> <url>` |\\n| Download without changing your branch | `grit fetch [<remote>]` |\\n| Fetch one branch or ref | `grit fetch origin main` (or `grit fetch origin pull/123/head:pr-123` for a PR) |\\n| Shallow fetch controls | `grit fetch --depth N`, `--deepen N`, or `--unshallow` (same semantics as Git; mutually exclusive) |\\n| Fetch and merge the remote copy of this branch | `grit pull` |\\n| Pull only when a fast-forward is possible | `grit pull --ff-only` (exit **0** on success, **1** with `refused_not_ff` when diverged; branch unchanged) |\\n| Fetch and replay local commits on top of upstream | `grit pull --rebase` (or `pull.rebase` in config; rebase conflicts abort atomically, exit **1**) |\\n| Replay local commits onto another base | `grit rebase [<upstream>]` (default `@{upstream}`; `--onto`; `--check` predicts conflicts without side effects; `-X ours\\\\|theirs` matches `git rebase -X` where **ours** is onto/upstream; stop on conflicts with `--continue` / `--abort` / `--skip`, exit **1**) |\\n| Publish this branch to `origin` under the same name | `grit push` |\\n| Push to a different remote branch name | `grit push origin HEAD:refs/heads/<name>` |\\n| Publish rewritten history safely | `grit fetch`, then `grit push --force-with-lease` (or `--force-with-lease=<rev>` for one ref); stale lease → exit **1**, `\\\"rejected\\\": true` |\\n| Publish all local tags | `grit push --tags` |\\n\\nOrdinary pushes must fast-forward the remote. If the remote has commits you don't, grit rejects the push (exit **1**, `\\\"rejected\\\": true`); run `grit pull`, then push again. After `grit history` edits (reword, drop, fixup, squash, split, rebase) or `grit rebase`, fetch and use `--force-with-lease` instead of `--force` so you don't overwrite someone else's work. `--force` overwrites unconditionally when you really need it.\\n\\nAfter a successful history rewrite (drop, move, reword, fixup, squash, absorb, or rebase apply), grit stores the ref plan in `.git/grit/history-last`. `grit history undo` restores those refs when tips are unchanged; run it again to redo. Use `--dry-run` to preview ref movements. Undo refuses when a ref moved or local changes would be overwritten.\\n\\nURLs can be `https://`, `ssh://`, `user@host:path`, `git://`, `file://` or a local path.\\n\\n## Bundles and linked worktrees\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Offline pack transfer | `grit bundle create`, `verify`, `list`, `unbundle` |\\n| Extra working trees | `grit worktree list`, `add`, `remove`, `lock`, `unlock`, `prune` |\\n\\n## Tags and config\\n\\n- `grit tag` lists tags (default `--sort=version`). Filters: `-l/--list <pattern>` (repeatable), `--sort version|date|name`, `--reverse`, `--contains [REV]`, `--points-at <REV>`, `-n` / `--limit`. List `--json` includes `target_kind` and `annotation` (subject, message, tagger, date) on annotated tags; human output shows the subject beside the name.\\n- `grit tag <name> [<target>]` creates a lightweight tag at `HEAD` or `<target>`. `-m` / `-F` create an annotated tag; `-f` replaces an existing tag; `--dry-run` previews create/delete. `grit tag -d <name>` deletes one. `grit tag --verify <name>…` checks annotated tag signatures and optional `tree-sha256` claims (exit 1 if any fail); `--no-signature` skips signature checks.\\n- `grit config <key>` reads, `grit config <key> <value>` sets, `grit config --unset <key>` removes, `grit config --list` lists. Add `--global` for the per-user file.\\n\\n## Staying non-interactive\\n\\n- HTTPS GitHub: run `grit auth` once in an interactive session (it uses a browser device flow). After that, fetch, pull and push use the stored token. When stdin isn't a terminal, grit won't prompt to sign in; it fails with a hint instead.\\n- SSH: set `GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes'` so a missing key or unknown host fails fast instead of waiting for input.\\n- Don't run `grit update` in automation; it reinstalls the binary.\\n- `grit manager`, `grit upload-pack` and `grit receive-pack` are plumbing that speaks Git protocols on stdin/stdout. Don't call them directly.\\n\\n## What grit doesn't do\\n\\ngrit has no equivalent of `rebase`, `reset`, interactive commands, or annotated or signed tags. Partial staging and discard use `--hunks`, `--lines`, and `--patch-file` on `add`, `restore`, `commit`, and `stash push`, not Git's `-p` / `-i`. During a merge conflict, use `grit restore --ours|--theirs|--merge` to compare sides or restore markers, then `grit merge --continue` (or `grit commit` for Git-compatible finish). Use `grit commit --amend` (optional message, JSON `amended` field) to rewrite the tip commit. For `grit stash pop` or `grit stash apply` with conflicts, use `git` to finish resolving. When a task needs something else on this list, use `git` on the same repository and come back to `grit` afterwards.\\n\"}\n",
